  "base_commit": "01608ff3ba33f6b4887bf76bef4ec01627e1b39b",
  "pr_number": 10594,
  "issue_number": 10593,
  "issue_title": "Cloning signatures shares callback options across workflows",
  "issue_body": "Cloning a signature with a linked callback leaves the callback's execution options shared across the original template and its clones. Stamping or freezing one callback can therefore alter another workflow's callback metadata and task identity.\n\nPrior discussion: [REPLACE_WITH_DISCUSSION_URL](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions/10592)\n\n# Checklist\n\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs) on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues) for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls) for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main) to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues in this issue.\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\nThe minimal reproduction below needs neither a broker connection nor pytest-celery. Additional validation used Celery's built-in `celery.contrib.pytest` fixtures and `celery.contrib.testing.worker.start_worker`, not the separate Docker-based pytest-celery plugin.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the Celery version and the output of `celery report` in the issue.\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of `pip freeze` in the issue (collected with `uv pip freeze`; the local editable path is replaced with its revision).\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries, ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\nThe broker-free example exercises none of the scheduling or retry features listed above.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #2251: earlier discussion of deep-copying signatures, including why task arguments should not be unconditionally deep-copied. This report concerns linked execution options.\n- #10561: merged fix for top-level kwargs aliasing, already present in the tested main revision. It does not isolate a linked Signature's options.\n- #10163: open optimization for copying scalar options. Its stated scope retains deep-copy behavior for nested mutable options.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\nNo matching open implementation found in the issue/PR searches. The related reports above have different scopes.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version**: main at `3e40f4332479ccd83527908dc9249c54531380de`, reporting package version 5.6.2. Reproduced on CPython 3.12.10, Windows 11.\n\nThe minimal example only constructs signatures. The separate worker check uses a solo worker, `memory://` broker, and `cache+memory://` result backend. Report collected with those transport settings:\n\n<details>\n<summary><code>celery report</code> output</summary>\n\n```text\nsoftware -> celery:5.6.2 (recovery) kombu:5.6.2 py:3.12.10\n            billiard:4.2.4 memory:N/A\nplatform -> system:Windows arch:64bit, WindowsPE\n            kernel version:11 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.default.Loader\nsettings -> transport:memory results:cache+memory:///\n\ndeprecated_settings: None\n```\n\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n- **Minimal Python Version**: unknown; tested with CPython 3.12.10.\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: unknown; tested against the main revision above.\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: unknown; installed version 5.6.2.\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: not required for the minimal example.\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: not required for the minimal example.\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: unknown; tested on Windows 11.\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: not required for the minimal example.\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: not required for the minimal example.\n\n### Python Packages\n\n<details>\n<summary><code>uv pip freeze</code> output, with the editable Celery path replaced by its revision</summary>\n\n```text\namqp==5.3.1\nattrs==26.1.0\nbilliard==4.2.4\n# celery==5.6.2, editable main at 3e40f4332479ccd83527908dc9249c54531380de\nclick==8.5.0\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.3.0\ncolorama==0.4.6\nflake8==7.3.0\niniconfig==2.3.0\nisort==9.0.1\nkombu==5.6.2\nmccabe==0.7.0\nmypy-extensions==1.1.0\npackaging==26.3\npluggy==1.6.0\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.53\npycodestyle==2.14.0\npyflakes==3.4.0\npygments==2.21.0\npytest==9.1.1\npytest-subtests==0.15.0\npytest-timeout==2.4.0\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\nsix==1.17.0\ntzdata==2026.3\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\nwcwidth==0.8.3\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n\nNone for the minimal example. No running broker, backend, database, or worker is required.\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\nRun against the main revision above:\n\n```python\nfrom celery import Celery, signature\n\nwith Celery(\"clone_repro\", broker=\"memory://\", fixups=[]) as app:\n    callback = signature(\"tasks.record\", app=app)\n    template = signature(\"tasks.process\", app=app)\n    template.link(callback)\n    first = template.clone()\n    second = template.clone()\n\n    first.stamp(workflow_id=\"first\")\n    second.stamp(workflow_id=\"second\")\n    first_callback = signature(first.options[\"link\"][0], app=app)\n    second_callback = signature(second.options[\"link\"][0], app=app)\n    print(\n        \"stamps:\",\n        callback.options.get(\"workflow_id\"),\n        first_callback.options.get(\"workflow_id\"),\n        second_callback.options.get(\"workflow_id\"),\n    )\n\n    first_callback.freeze()\n    second_callback.freeze()\n    print(\"callback IDs reused:\", first_callback.id == second_callback.id)\n    print(\"template callback frozen:\", callback.id is not None)\n```\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nEach clone owns its callback's execution options. Stamping and freezing those callbacks should leave the template untouched:\n\n```text\nstamps: None first second\ncallback IDs reused: False\ntemplate callback frozen: False\n```\n\nExplicit task IDs already present in a template should remain preserved; this example starts with an unfrozen callback.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\n```text\nstamps: second second second\ncallback IDs reused: True\ntemplate callback frozen: True\n```\n\n`Signature.clone()` deep-copies its options, but a nested `Signature.__deepcopy__()` returns a shallow dictionary and memoizes the original Signature. The nested callback options therefore remain shared. Equivalent checks reproduce the aliasing for `link_error` and a Signature in the `chord` option; already-serialized dictionary counterparts are isolated correctly.\n\nA separate real worker check also recorded `[('first', 'second'), ('second', 'second')]` for `(workflow, callback stamp)`. With a focused local fix to deep-copy only execution options and memoize the copied dictionary, it records `[('first', 'first'), ('second', 'second')]`, and the original template callback options remain empty.\n\nRegression tests fail on the original implementation (9 failed, 9 passed in the affected test file). With the local fix, the three related Canvas/stamping test files report 672 passed, 2 xfailed, and 27,627 subtests passed. Task arguments, including lazy iterators, retain their existing copying behavior. These are local results; the full CI and external-broker matrix have not been run.\n\nAI assistance: Codex assisted with investigation, patch preparation, and regression tests. The reported results were obtained by running the code locally.\n",
